{"name":"Perfection","hex":"#d9d6e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"perfection","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quality or state of being perfect or complete, so that nothing substandard remains; the highest attainable state or degree of excellence.","A quality, endowment, or acquirement completely excellent; an ideal; faultlessness; especially, the divine attribute of complete excellence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To perfect."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/perfection"}],"entries":[{"title":"Perfection","blocks":[{"text":"Perfection is a state, variously, of completeness, flawlessness, or supreme excellence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Disc_Plain_black.svg/330px-Disc_Plain_black.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfection","wikidata":"Q378701"},{"title":"Perfection (board game)","blocks":[{"text":"Perfection is a game originally produced by the Pennsylvania company Reed Toys and then by the Milton Bradley company. The object is to put all the pieces into matching holes on the board before the time limit runs out. When time runs out, the board springs up, causing the pieces to fly out. In the most common version, there are 25 pieces to be placed into a 5×5 grid within 60 seconds."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfection_(board_game)","wikidata":"Q7168111"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg/500px-Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg/1280px-Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg/500px-Aristoteles_Louvre.jpg?utm_source=en.w